ective perhaps readily available test  unfortunately a lot of these tests were  not fully available in fact the  department of health and human services  actually sequestered a lot of these  tests so  early on these weren't available and  they were targeted towards  communities and some health systems and  also retirement homes and  nursing facilities of skilled nursing  facilities  so we also recognize as we tested  populations and perhaps  these weren't the best populations these  could have been low risk populations  uh they were prone to false positives so  even if these tests had a 99 or 98  specificity uh when the prevalence was  low your positive predictive value isn't  so great right so we can do the math  there  and and see why that is a problem uh but  we do see that later  point of care anagen test this is from  our colleagues at uc san francisco  they show here that these um and these  tests are were able to um  do reasonably well and when we actually  evaluated their sensitivity  relative to what's called a ct cutoff so  if you recall from pcr testing  real-time pcr reports ct values which  are cycle threshold values  and the bigger the ct value the um the  lower the viral load is right there  so if you're able to kind of put engine  testing and pcr testing on so-called  more even  ground to compare we may have to define  a ct cutoff so that  we would know when perhaps a person is  likely to be antigen  negative relative to pcr because they  are perhaps convalescing and there's  less viral load  where there's no more proteins that are  present the virus isn't producing as  much proteins  replicating as well and therefore that's  why the antigen is lower  if you look at data from the cochrane  report there's actually quite a recent  review here and this is from march 24th  they looked at uh quite a few uh studies  so in this case for symptomatic patients  being tested by antigen testing you see  from 37 uh  uh 37 evaluations consisting of 27  different studies  and summing about over 15 000 samples  you can see that  the the overall sensitivity of these  tests are about 72 percent  specificity is reasonably good at 99.5  percent  but the other thing i want to call your  attention to is asymptomatic testing  and this is where the focus has been now  because we are talking about reopening  schools  businesses reopening whole states whole  cities  and the asymptomatic population is where  we worry and  unfortunately you can see here that the  sensitivity is at about 58  uh specificity remains reasonably good  so at 98.9 percent but  sensitivity did suffer in this cohort  uh if you look at the types of tests  that were looked at you can again see  the  symptomatic versus asymptomatic patients  you can see the sensitivity and  specifics do vary by different platforms  too  uh and you can see in this case amongst  the asymptomatic population  one antigen test appears to have at best  a 69.2  sensitivity when compared against pcr  so what does all this data tell us uh  and where do we need to go from here  and this data tells us that we do need  better antigen tests the point of care  tests  have a role i'm not trying to discount  that that they may not perform well they  have a role we need to test in the right  populations  but we now are coming to an age of  laboratory-based antigen tests and  how does this change our our  understanding  and appreciation for deploying these  tests in contrast to point-of-care  antigen tests as i've  shown you which can only really do one  test at the time  laboratory-based antigen tests are are  going to be high throughput  they're going to have scalable reagent  supply they are going to perhaps be  lower cost due to economy  due to economies of scale why do i  believe that well  for those that operate in a clinical  laboratory specifically in clinical  chemistry  you know how many thyroid hormones you  run you know how many  vitamin ds you run you know how many  cardiac troponins you run and  manufacturers can scale  to that reagent level and so just for  context we could be looking at tens of  thousands of tests  per day they are definitely cheap in  some cases we could have  only a few dollars per test so you can  see here that there could be some  potential improvement in sensitivity  because we're using perhaps  lab laboratory based analyzer so that  may be of value compared to point of  care test but what's also valuable with  these laboratory-based tests is they  don't use proprietary media so  what i failed to mention earlier was  these point-of-care antigen tests  very convenient very fast but they often  use  a proprietary media that required you to  pretty much  add some reagents which pretty much make  them not feasible to be rerun by pcr  again in contrast some molecular  platforms may be able to use  a residual sample from laboratory-based  antigen tests and these are things that  are very exciting as we move to the next  phase of testing  during the pandemic so the pros and cons  uh here really for high throughput  lab-based antigen testing for cobin 19  you could be looking at 3 000 tests per  day on  instrument if you have the right  instrument the sense the claim  sensitivity specificity has been  reported to be about 97  sensitivity greater than 99 specificity  reagent supply seems to be stable  sufficient volumes  could these sufficient volumes of  capacity to make a noticeable dent  in testing deficiencies in hospitals  the sensitivity is acceptable for these  populations and could be used for lower  risk  patients the cons however a lot of these  tests require you to add some sort of  reagent a license buffer or stabilizing  buffer  sometimes it's within a very short  period of time so within 12 hours of  collections others we are seeing at 24  hours of collection  you can have staffing limitations who's  going to be the one that aliquots these  pipettes in these reagents to stabilize  the  samples right so you may have to think  about that if you lack automation or  automation that can do that  uh there is a shortage of serial pipette  tips at the time of putting together  this slide it's still not that great  and you should be using sterile tips and  um and this would affect if you don't  use sterile chips you can't reflex the  pcr right you could be introducing  contaminants  uh reagent stability that's going to be  ever evolving as manufacturers roll out  new tests you see that they will  have shorter stability and of course as  time evolves you'll hope that they will  have a longer stability time  so at uc davis health we are very lucky  to have access to  a previously uh laboratory-based energy  test which now has received emergency  use authorization  and in full disclosure we brought this  test and evaluated it  mainly because we were very concerned in  december and january that our pcr  supplies were dropping too quickly due  to that last surge  so this laboratory antigen test was  going to be  our backup plan uh fortunately or  unfortunately we never deployed it  so we were able to have some good data  to see how well it worked compared to  our pcr platform  but you can see here that we looked at  all of symptomatic and asymptomatic  patients that compared them all combined  too  but we also applied that ct value cutoff  and we looked at  comparing without a ct value cut off we  looked at comparing with a ct value of  33  we looked at comparing against a ct  value of 30 on pcr  and you can remember just higher ct  values mean  lower viral load and what's important to  appreciate here  is that is that on the bottom right hand  corner  we are ultimately um we are ultimately  uh having a 95  uh sensitivity and 90 um specificity in  this case  so ultimately uh this is reasonably good  and as this only occurs when you have a  ct  value of 30. so this is  an important point to appreciate you  look at ct value of 33. it's not too bad  either a sensitivity of 92.7  uh for symptomatic patients and  asymptomatic um  has 78 this isn't isn't too bad  and it actually uh allows us to evaluate  um  the uh the actual um process for  actual uh using in these populations to  screen for patient populations  so with that said we ultimately  appreciate here that these tests could  be employed if we  can come to terms with using ct cutoffs  as  a means to put pcr on the same  plane as antigen testing this leaves us  also the discussion that  can a person who's antigen negative a  pcr positive with a high ct  value be actually  infectious enough or perhaps less  infectious no one has an  answer to that question yet but you can  perhaps  feel that as a provocative hypothesis  because of the fact that if the virus  viral proteins aren't there  are the whole viruses there at all we  don't know that  and there are challenges in how we can  test for that with viral culture and so  forth because  there are limits to that technology as  well  regardless as we look at how cdc guides  us in this case they went with  a prevalence and pretest probability  based  algorithm so you can see here if you are  symptomatic and you are antigen positive  they're going to say that you are  infected with cov2  so they're not going to require you to  confirm that energy result however if  you're symptomatic and antigen negative  you may need to actually follow up with  some sort of nucleic acid amplification  test  next in the middle column you can see if  you're asymptomatic and have close  contact with covid19  chances are if you had someone that was  near you with cobit 19  you're more likely to um actually uh  catch it right so therefore if you're  antigen negative you're  you're likely to be truly negative if  you're you're positive then they would  recommend that you confirm  by naat lastly and on the right most  column you can see that asymptomatic  patients with no known exposure  obviously if your antigen positive let's  see if that's a false positive or not so  they're going to need you to reconfirm  if you're energy negative then it's  likely that you are not infected  with covet 19 or stars cov2  so in conclusion the kovid 19 pandemic  has created unique challenges globally  with laboratory minutes in playing a  central role diagnostic testing consists  of molecular and engine testing  engine testing is slowly emerging as a  potential low-cost  adjunct to molecular solutions early  sars cov2 engine tests exhibit  low sensitivity and specificity things  are improving  such as these newer laboratory based  tests that we are seeing especially as  we assess them  in more even grounds with looking at  their ct values when they're compared  against  pcr our best practices right now as far  as kobe 2 engine testing should rely on  pre-test probability as defined by cdc  guidelines  but the technology and the science  continues to evolve  and so forth so we'll have to wait and  see but we do see a valuable role of  energy testing you just have to use it  in the right  population so with that said i'll close  out and thank you everyone for your time  and i'm now happy to answer any  questions  and thank you so much dr tran for that  informative presentation  we will now start the live q and a  portion of the webinar  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  so let's take a look it looks like we  already have a few questions  that have come in so dr tran  is pcr testing screening for dx  kobe 2 etc the only real option  given its recognition to the gold  standard  and i think that that's a great question  so i would probably  say there there's um a couple answers to  that it really depends on what you want  to do with that result right  so for instance if it's uh if we we  definitely need to act on a result  meaning that we're gonna give you a  therapeutic  you know a life-saving therapy we're  trying to figure out why you are super  sick and needing to be admitted  yeah molecular testing should be the  primary means to do that  if you are talking about um needing to  get tested to go  fly on a trip there's a lot of  discussion  with that so various organizations the  airline industry and so forth and  different countries have a lot of  opinions on this  right now they're saying it's gonna be  molecular testing of some kind i don't  believe as of yet  antigen testing is accepted uh for  um as a pre-test and return pre-return  uh screening uh tool for testing um  other perspectives however if you're  really just looking at  seeing how um your risk for having  covid19  based on what i showed you on those cdc  guidelines energy testing may work a  good example of this is  our use of antigen point of care testing  as part of migrant worker testing at uc  davis so  these are people that don't have the um  the financial means  the travel means or really the time to  come to a hospital to get tested with  our fancy molecular tests  however there's a lot of these people  out there and we're called diagnostic or  testing deserts as it were  and these are high-risk communities so  we decided to deploy  engine testing out there because there's  no real cost effective solution  for molecular point of care testing for  these populations so in this case we use  antigen for that and that does  play a role thank you so much now what  do you see  as a biggest challenge with antigen  testing for cov2  i think that the two challenges um are  first off  whatever we decide to do we have to go  all in we can't have  a situation where some people decide to  use antigen some don't  and and for any pandemic related  solutions  everyone has has to be rowing in the  same direction to use that analogy  otherwise it's going to be piecemeal and  unfortunately a lot of the early  pandemic  response was that and that's because of  sheer desperation right people were  looking for different types of tests  now the second issue and i feel this is  the bigger barrier  is really uh health care health sciences  we've all been pro including me  we were all programmed that molecular  testing is the way to go  and in some cases it's extremely hard to  break uh healthcare practitioners away  from what they've been trained  and i understand it because that you  know that the potential for a 10 to 15  false negative rate does scare people  uh however how like i said many times in  this presentation  it's who you're testing because if you  talk to a population where there's  hundreds of thousands of people in a  testing desert where  zero people or very few people have  access to testing  that eighty-five percent sensitivity or  even seventy percent sensitive  starts looking darn good uh because  that means that i'm catching a majority  of people that would have the disease to  prevent spread to  others so i think the challenges with  antigen testing is we are victims  of of having been ingrained in the the  pcr world the molecular world but also  at the same time  it it's going to be hard for people to  get away from the news stories  where early antigen tests were used  probably in the wrong populations  and causing false positive false  negative results and it has incurred  a perhaps less than ideal name and that  can be a barrier to adoption  at various facilities  thank you so much and we have one more  question from our audience  where do you see in lab high throughput  antigen testing  fitting in as we move toward forward in  the pandemic  i think as prevalence goes down that  will help a lot and i think as people  start realizing how much it costs right  now because you know early on some tests  were subsidized some tests were  augmented by various means  uh but when it's still um you know  double digits or plus  or three triple digits for a pcr test  uh people it starts adding up and  and i think as the prevalence goes down  we have a handle on the virus and the  majority of people are vaccinated  energy test starts looking real good  because of the fact that they are going  to be inherently  cheaper they are fast the data for it  looks good as long as using the right  population and  i think that's where it's going to be  let's put into context  hiv testing i know you know this is not  covid but  for context and perhaps i'm comparing  apples and oranges  because obviously hiv doesn't have a you  know a 30  asymptomatic population to worry about  it's not spread by  airborne droplets but at the same time  you look at the primary means to screen  for hiv is actually antibodies and  antigen  the molecular test is expensive we only  do it to effectively confirm and look at  viral load  so all these things are things to  consider as we shift to the post  pandemic world how do we be able to  cost effectively administer  testing for large populations as part of  routine care pcr is not going to go away  it will be used for confirmation and  perhaps still testing high-risk patients  but antigen i feel has a role especially  the lab-based platforms because they  have just so much capacity  and can do so cost effectively  dr tran thank you so much for your time  today would you like to provide me  closing remarks for our audience before  we go  sure well first i want to thank everyone  for taking the time to listen but also  you know  i think just stick with it you know i've  been here since uh you know  dealing with covid since february 26 and  probably a couple weeks even before that  of 2020  but the reality is you got to stay with  it because the science continues to  evolve and we have to remain flexible  and open-minded  in terms of what we do to continue to  fight the pandemic  absolutely and thank you again dr tran  for your presentation  and for your important research and i  also want to thank the lab roots and our  sponsor dia soren for underwriting  today's  educational webcast and before we go i  wan